<title>SWITZERLAND: Denmark asks U.N. to censure China on rights.</title>
<headline>Denmark asks U.N. to censure China on rights.</headline>
<dateline>GENEVA 1997-04-10</dateline>
<text>
<p>Denmark on Thursday formally presented the U.N. Human Rights Commission with a Western-backed resolution criticising China's rights record.</p>
<p>Danish delegation head Tyge Lehmann submitted a draft text co-sponsored by the United States and 13 other countries, including 10 European Union member states.  </p>
<p>&quot;We have 15 countries,&quot; Lehmann told reporters, adding that more countries could still co-sponsor the text before the Commission votes next Tuesday. &quot;We hope to have 30.&quot;</p>
<p>&quot;I think some governments are very reluctant to put their name on the resolution, but have promised certainly to support it (when voting),&quot; the Danish rights delegation head said.</p>
<p>The 15 official sponsors -- who could still be joined by other states as co-sponsors until Tuesday -- are: Austria, Belgium, Britain, Denmark, Finland, Iceland, Ireland, Liechtenstein, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway, Portugal, Sweden, Switzerland and the United States.</p>
<p>But diplomatic sources said support for the Western motion seemed to be eroding in the 53-member body, making it likely Beijing escapes censure again for the seventh straight year. China is trying to rally backing for its own &quot;no-action motion&quot; which would pre-empt any vote on Denmark's text.</p>
</text>
